Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Gender: Likely no strong direct influence on whether the student reads academic books most of the time.
- Age: Older undergraduates might read more academically due to increased course difficulty or specialization.
- Department: Students from certain departments (e.g., Pharmacy, CSE) may be more likely to read academic books often than others (e.g., BBA, English for leisure reading).
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More hours likely correlate with reading academic books most of the time.
- Reason for reading books: "Academic Purpose" strongly suggests reading academic books most of the time.
- How to manage books for reading: Methods like "Used-all" or "Buy" might indicate serious academic reading habits.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher numbers may indicate more academic reading if combined with other features.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Similarly, higher numbers likely indicate academic reading.
- Favorite books for leisure: If mostly fiction or politics, less likely academic reading most of the time.
- What is the change felt after reading books: "Increase Academic Performance" or "Increase Problem Solving Skill" suggests academic reading.
- Who influenced you to read books: "Teachers" or "Self-Decision" may indicate more academic reading.
- Which language books do you like to read: English might be more common for academic books, but not definitive.
- Where do you read printed books: Reading in the library likely correlates with academic reading.
- Do you read Newspaper: Possibly unrelated or less relevant.
- The format used for reading books: "Both" or "Printed" might be more common for academic reading.
- Frequency of reading books: "On everyday basis" strongly suggests frequent academic reading.

